---
description: AI agent development rules for wow-converter
alwaysApply: false
---
- Avoid using `any` or `as` casting in TypeScript unless absolutely necessary. Avoid unncessary `instanceof` when developing in TypeScript, trust TypeScript static type.
- **Architecture decisions:** recorded in `docs/decisions/` (ADR-style). Check there before assuming Go/TS parity or re-porting Go-only features to TypeScript.
- Do not export functions, types, or constants that are not used outside their module/directory. Only export what is needed by external consumers.
- Do not extract helpers into separate util files when they have a single caller **and** the caller file stays small — keep them in the caller file as unexported functions. When the owner module is already large, colocate by **folder** (same package/directory as the owner) rather than inflating a single file.
- **Go colocation:** keep helpers next to their caller/owner. Do not put domain logic in generic packages (`config/constants`, shared `util`, etc.). If multiple packages need it, export from the canonical owner file and have consumers import that package (e.g. API → `mapexporter` for ADT tile concurrency). Reserve `config` for real configuration constants only. Tiny helpers used by one primitive (e.g. `WorkerPool`, `Queue`) stay unexported in that primitive's file; duplicate at most one other primitive boundary instead of centralizing.
- Code organization: In files, export exported functions/types first, then unexported/internal functions. For classes, order methods as: public/no modifier → protected → private.
- Refactoring patterns:
  - Consolidate multiple related properties into unified data structures (e.g., single `Asset[]` array instead of separate `usedImportPaths`, `unusedTextures`, etc.).
  - If a method doesn't use `this`, make it a standalone function below the class, not a class method.
  - Remove duplication by consolidating similar loops/operations into single iterations with shared logic.
  - Organize complex methods into clear phases with comments (e.g., "1) Discover X", "2) Process Y", "3) Compute Z").
  - Prefer local helper functions over repeated inline logic; extract when logic appears 2+ times.
  - Only store shared state as class properties; move method-local data into local variables.
- Run the default bundled Go app with `npm run dev` as a background daemon emitting logs to `log.txt`. Use `npm run dev:ts` only when explicitly testing the legacy TS server. When `Serving UI web interface at http://127.0.0.1:3001/` appears, the server started successfully. Always kill old servers on ports 3000, 3001, and 17753 before starting. The Go converter and web UI hot-reload; the bundled in-process wow-data-server restarts with the converter.
- Screenshots are mandatory in PR to provide proof of change correctness. Use Playwright MCP to make screenshot under `.playwright-mcp/`; copy into `docs/` before commit. Every change in PR must have a corresponding screenshot.
- When taking a screenshot of an exported model, run `go -C golang run ./cmd/shot-converter <asset-path>` (server on port 3001) and read the PNG it prints. Optional `--seq Attack --view front`. For a Wowhead comparison, add `--wowhead <url> --wow-seq <WoW-name>` to capture both sources with the server's camera transfer. Follow both shot skills and read both PNGs.
- API integration tests call a converter that is already running (`bun dev` or the unpacked desktop app). `bun run test:api` uses `WOW_CONVERTER_URL` or `http://127.0.0.1:3001`. The suite does not start a server.
- Visual snapshots guard conversion changes. The catalog is `RetailCases` and `ClassicCases` in `golang/internal/testcases/cases.go`. Retail cases use the local `wow` product. Classic cases use `wow_classic`. The runner switches the loaded product and restores the one it found at start.
  - Clear exported assets between products: identical texture paths can have different bytes in Retail and Classic. Raw cache clearing alone does not isolate exported textures.
  - Run with `bun run test:snapshot` against the converter already listening at `WOW_CONVERTER_URL` or `http://127.0.0.1:3001`. One case: `SNAPSHOT_SUITE=retail SNAPSHOT_SLUG=<slug> bun test tests/snapshot-tests/model/model.test.ts`. `bun test` does not forward arguments after `--`.
  - Update with `bun run test:snapshot-update` (`SNAPSHOT_UPDATE=1`). One case: `$env:SNAPSHOT_UPDATE='1'; $env:SNAPSHOT_SUITE='retail'; $env:SNAPSHOT_SLUG='<slug>'; bun test tests/snapshot-tests/model/model.test.ts` (PowerShell). Unix: prefix env vars. Update rewrites the expected image and the recorded sequence. It keeps `diff.thresholdPercent` and `diff.channelDelta`.
  - A snapshot run writes `review.html` (wowhead | expected | actual). Open `tests/snapshot-tests/model/review.html`. Dropdown defaults to failed; switch to successful. Eligible Wowhead URL cases (no attached weapons, mount suite excluded) capture `<slug>.wowhead.png` when missing; `shot.cameras` is stored for reference and unused by expected/actual. A Wowhead capture failure is skipped. 👍/👎 sorts into good/bad (bad asks for a comment); Copy results. Re-run `bun tests/snapshot-tests/model/_write-review.ts` to rebuild from `review-failures.json`, or pass `suite/slug` args.
  - Case files live in `tests/snapshot-tests/model/<suite>/<slug>/`, including `<slug>.actual.png`, `<slug>.diff.png`, and `<slug>.expected-prev.png`. Those three plus `review.html` and `review-failures.json` are gitignored.
  - Each view fits the mesh vertices to 92% of the tile on the limiting axis. A tall model fills the height; a top view fits the footprint.
  - The sheet is 2×3, tile 640×400: front, left, back / right, top, bottom. A pixel counts when its largest channel change is greater than `channelDelta` (default 4). Fail when counted/sheet exceeds `thresholdPercent` (default 1). A sequence other than `shot.sequence`, or case fields that differ from `cases.go`, also fail. A missing expected image fails; do not create one on a compare run.
  - `<slug>.diff.png` is black where the sheets match. A changed pixel is red, and a larger channel change is a stronger red. Read it on failure. After an intentional change, update that slug and review the new expected image before committing it.
  - Reported models that do not have an approved sheet yet have an `issue` string in the manifest, `<slug>.reference.png` (the wow.export shot), and `<slug>.reported.png` (the shot of our export from that report). Compare still writes `<slug>.actual.png` and does not pixel-diff them. Compare `actual.png` to `reference.png`. A full update does not write `expected.png` for those cases. Set `SNAPSHOT_SLUG` to approve that sheet.
- When working with a model, read and write `.mdl` text (Materials, Textures, Geosets, Sequences), for inspection and edits as well as debugging. Export `format: "mdl"`, or call `MDL.ToMdl()`. Do not hex-edit, grep, or diff MDX bytes.
- Documentation updates: before raising PR, always incorporate maintainer feedback into this file promptly. Keep it extremely compact and high-signal (short bullets over long prose).
- Agentic loop: when running temporary Bun scripts, remember to put process.exit(0) so that the background process terminates instead of hanging indefinitely.
- When describing tooling or workflows, avoid referencing removed/legacy scripts or past decisions.
- **Prose economy:** mention a detail only when it is relevant. Negatives are fine when they answer a real question. Do not bring up a concern nobody had and then explain it does not apply. Say "we don't do X" only when readers would reasonably expect X.
